<Doc title="DFPlayer Mini" subtitle="serial MP3 player, DIP-16" id="dfplayerMini">

<Section title="What it is">
<Text>A self-contained MP3 player on a 20 mm square board. It holds its own
microSD card, decodes on the module, and drives a small speaker directly - so
the host says <Code>play track 3</Code> over a UART and nothing else.</Text>

<Text>For <Bold>bibo</Bold> this is the thing that fills the hole in the cue
system. <Code>cue::soundWrite()</Code> has recorded a tone and driven nothing
since the day it was written, because there was no buzzer and no pin set aside
for one. A DFPlayer is better than a buzzer: a cue can say a <Bold>word</Bold>
rather than a beep.</Text>
</Section>

<Pinout title="Pinout" package="DIP-16" pitch="2.54 mm">
<Note>Pin 1 is the corner nearest the microSD slot, marked with a dot. The left
column runs 1 to 8 downward and the right column runs 16 down to 9, which is
how a DIP is numbered and how this board reads on the bench.</Note>

<Column side="left" from="1" to="8">
<Pin n="1" name="VCC"   class="power">3.2 - 5.0 V. 100 uF across the supply.</Pin>
<Pin n="2" name="RX"    class="serial">UART in. 9600 baud, 10-byte frames.</Pin>
<Pin n="3" name="TX"    class="serial">UART out.</Pin>
<Pin n="4" name="DAC_R" class="audio">Line out, right. About 0.6 V RMS.</Pin>
<Pin n="5" name="DAC_L" class="audio">Line out, left. Silkscreen may read DAC_I.</Pin>
<Pin n="6" name="SPK2"  class="audio" check="yes">Speaker, one side.</Pin>
<Pin n="7" name="GND"   class="ground">Power ground.</Pin>
<Pin n="8" name="SPK1"  class="audio" check="yes">Speaker, other side.</Pin>
</Column>

<Column side="right" from="16" to="9">
<Pin n="16" name="BUSY"   class="digital">LOW while playing, HIGH when idle.</Pin>
<Pin n="15" name="USB-"   class="usb">USB DM. Exposes the card to a PC.</Pin>
<Pin n="14" name="USB+"   class="usb">USB DP.</Pin>
<Pin n="13" name="ADKEY2" class="analog">Resistor-ladder keys. Plays track 5.</Pin>
<Pin n="12" name="ADKEY1" class="analog">Resistor-ladder keys. Plays track 1.</Pin>
<Pin n="11" name="IO2"    class="digital">Active low. Tap next, hold volume up.</Pin>
<Pin n="10" name="GND"    class="ground">Power ground.</Pin>
<Pin n="9"  name="IO1"    class="digital">Active low. Tap previous, hold volume down.</Pin>
</Column>
</Pinout>

<Section title="Two cells to check against your own board">
<Warn>The right column above matches the DFRobot pin table exactly, in the same
16-down-to-9 order it was read off the board - which is good evidence the board
is being read correctly. The left column has one disagreement.</Warn>

<Text>Pins 6 and 8 were read as <Code>SPK_1</Code> then <Code>SPK_2</Code>
downward. The DFRobot table has <Code>SPK2</Code> on 6 and <Code>SPK1</Code> on
8. Clone boards do vary, so the silkscreen wins over any table - including this
one.</Text>

<Text>It is worth knowing that this particular disagreement is
<Bold>harmless</Bold>. The speaker output is differential: the cone hangs
between SPK1 and SPK2 and neither is ground, so swapping them inverts the phase
and does nothing else. Nothing is at risk either way, which is exactly why it is
worth writing down rather than worrying about.</Text>

<Text>Pin 5 read as <Code>DAC_I</Code> is almost certainly <Code>DAC_L</Code> -
an uppercase L in the silkscreen font. It is the left channel.</Text>
</Section>

<Section title="Wiring it to bibo">
<List>
<Item><Bold>Power.</Bold> The module wants 3.2 - 5.0 V and takes current in
gulps when the amplifier is driven. Off the ESC's BEC rather than off the Pico,
with 100 uF across it - a supply that sags takes the Pico down with it.</Item>

<Item><Bold>RX needs a resistor from 5 V logic, and not from the Pico.</Bold>
The usual 1 kOhm in series exists because the module's RX is not 5 V tolerant.
The Pico is a 3.3 V part, so it is already the right level - the resistor is
cheap insurance against a wiring mistake, not a requirement here.</Item>

<Item><Bold>TX needs nothing.</Bold> The module drives 3.3 V, which the Pico
reads as high.</Item>

<Item><Bold>BUSY is worth wiring.</Bold> One GPIO tells the firmware whether a
cue is still being spoken, without asking over the UART. That is the same
question <Code>cue::busy()</Code> already answers for lamps.</Item>
</List>

<Warn>Do not connect the speaker outputs and the line outputs at the same time.
SPK1/SPK2 is a bridged amplifier and DAC_L/DAC_R is a line level tap; driving
both is a fight the amplifier wins and the module loses.</Warn>
</Section>

<Section title="Pins bibo does not have spare">
<Text>A UART is two pins and BUSY is a third, and the board is currently full.
GP0 and GP1 are the servo and the ESC. GP4 and GP5 are I2C. GP10 through GP15
are lamps, on borrowed ToF and encoder pins. GP16 through GP19 are the SD
header.</Text>

<Text>The permanent lamp move - <Code>GP2/GP3</Code> indicators,
<Code>GP6/GP7</Code> tails, <Code>GP8</Code> heads - frees GP10 through GP15 and
is what makes room for this. UART0 on GP12/GP13 or UART1 on GP8/GP9 both work
once the lamps have moved.</Text>
</Section>

</Doc>
